# What the moxy service account may run as root, on every node of the cluster.
#
# Comments are in English like the rest of the tooling; docs/DEPLOIEMENT.md and
# docs/adr/0010-node-maintenance-over-ssh.md explain the reasoning in French.
#
#   visudo -c -f deploy/moxy-maintenance.sudoers
#   install -D -o root -g root -m 0440 deploy/moxy-maintenance.sudoers \
#       /etc/sudoers.d/moxy-maintenance
#
# deploy/moxy-node-setup.sh does both, in that order: a sudoers file that does
# not parse is not an inert file, it is a broken sudo for every user of the
# node, so it gets checked before it is put in place and never after.
#
# The file name carries no dot and no tilde on purpose -- sudo skips those in
# /etc/sudoers.d, and a rule nobody reads fails open in the confusing direction.

Cmnd_Alias MOXY_MAINT = /usr/sbin/ha-manager crm-command node-maintenance enable *, \
                        /usr/sbin/ha-manager crm-command node-maintenance disable *

moxy ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: MOXY_MAINT

# THE * ABOVE IS NOT THE BARRIER, and reading it as one is the mistake this
# comment exists to prevent. sudo's wildcard is a shell-style pattern matched
# against the rest of the command line, and it matches spaces: the last
# argument it allows is therefore not "one node name" but "anything at all,
# including further words". Written alone, this rule would hand the moxy
# account every ha-manager crm-command node-maintenance invocation it cares to
# assemble.
#
# The real barrier is /usr/local/sbin/moxy-maintenance, and it works because it
# is the ONLY path by which the moxy account can reach sudo at all: the account
# has no shell (/usr/sbin/nologin), and sshd runs the validator and nothing else
# -- command="..." in authorized_keys, or force-command in the certificate. The
# validator accepts exactly two verbs and a node name matched against
# /etc/pve/nodes, so what reaches this file has already been checked by
# something that does not accept spaces.
#
# What sudoers contributes is the other half, and it is worth keeping: it closes
# the verb. Without the alias the grant would be "all of ha-manager" -- and
# ha-manager can move services, stop them, and rewrite the HA configuration of
# the cluster. A validator that someone later widens by one line would then
# widen the root grant with it; here it cannot, because the grant was never
# wider than these two commands.
